# Per-client rate limiting vs load shedding: two different questions (Google SRE book ch. 22; AWS Builders' Library, Yanacek 2019).
...@_style
direction: down
rl: "PER-CLIENT RATE LIMIT\nasks: is this client over its quota?\n(fairness between clients)" {
  class: boundary
  a: "client A\nwithin quota" {class: ok}
  b: "client B\nOVER quota" {class: bad}
  c: "client C\nwithin quota" {class: ok}
  lim: "limiter\nper client" {class: svc}
  a -> lim
  b -> lim: "429 for B only"
  c -> lim
}
ls: "LOAD SHEDDING\nasks: am I, the server, over capacity right now?" {
  class: boundary
  a: "client A\nwithin quota" {class: ok}
  b: "client B\nwithin quota" {class: ok}
  c: "client C\nwithin quota" {class: ok}
  srv: "server\nover capacity" {class: bad}
  a -> srv
  b -> srv
  c -> srv: "shed the excess"
}
rl -> ls: "you need both" {style.stroke-dash: 4}
why: "Even with throttling, servers still overload (AWS):\n· a surge in traffic\n· losing part of the fleet\n· clients shifting from cheap requests to expensive ones\nRate limiting \"may not be able to stop a failure that has already begun\" (Google SRE book)" {class: warn}
ls -> why {style.stroke-dash: 4}
